<figure class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-2 border-ink p-4 sm:p-6">
<p class="font-bold">A match that is rare among innocent people is not the same as guilt being certain.</p>
<div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-2 text-center text-base"><div class="border-2 border-ink p-3"><strong>100,000</strong><p>Possible suspects</p></div><div class="border-2 border-ink p-3"><strong>≈ 100</strong><p>Evidence matches</p></div><div class="border-2 border-ink p-3"><strong>1</strong><p>Actual culprit</p></div></div>
<svg class="w-full" viewBox="0 0 480 105" role="img" aria-labelledby="dna-title dna-desc"><title id="dna-title">One culprit among about a hundred matching people</title><desc id="dna-desc">A hundred cells show approximately ninety-nine innocent matching people and a single culprit.</desc>
{Array.from({length:100},(_,i)=><rect x={9+(i%25)*18.4} y={10+Math.floor(i/25)*18.4} width="13.4" height="13.4" fill={i===0?"#387a91":"#bd4b37"} fill-opacity={i===0?1:0.45}/>)}
</svg>
<figcaption class="text-base leading-normal">Suppose the trace matches 1 in 1,000 people, with 100,000 equally plausible possible suspects. Around 100 people would match; only one is the culprit. With those simplified assumptions, a matching suspect has about a 1% chance of guilt, not 99.9%. The match is useful evidence, but it is not the same as the probability of guilt.</figcaption></figure>